VEX map 5 (AMX-FP8 / AMX-TRANSPOSE) instructions decoded one byte too long

decoder_vex64's immediate rule is written against the flattened opcode table
index: map 1 occupies 0x000-0x0FF, map 2 0x100-0x1FF, map 3 0x200-0x2FF and,
with BX_SUPPORT_AMX, map 5 0x300-0x3FF and map 7 0x400-0x4FF. The test

  has_immediate = (0x70..0x73) || (0xC2..0xC6) || (opcode_byte >= 0x200)

was meant as "map 3 always has an imm8, map 7 an imm32", but it also catches
every map 5 opcode, none of which carries an immediate (TDPBF8PS / TDPHF8PS /
TDPBHF8PS / TDPHBF8PS at FD, T2RPNTLVWZ0RS/1RS and their T1 twins at F8/F9).
The decoder therefore consumed one extra byte and advanced RIP by 6 for a
5-byte instruction: the AMX-FP8 dot product itself executed correctly, and
the byte after it was silently swallowed — in a straight-line sequence the
NEXT instruction was then decoded from its second byte (a following
`tilerelease` C4 E2 78 49 C0 turned into `loop` E2 78).

decoder_evex64 (and the 32-bit VEX decoder, which rejects maps >= 4) already
bound the range with `< 0x300`; the 64-bit VEX rule now does the same and
names map 7 explicitly for its imm32.

Fixed in decoder/fetchdecode64.cc. Found 2026-09-14 by diffing an independent
model of AMX-FP8: the RCX decrement of the phantom `loop` showed up in the
register diff while the tile result matched.

diff --git a/cpu/decoder/fetchdecode64.cc b/cpu/decoder/fetchdecode64.cc
index 9cf70ed..d65c1aa 100644
--- a/cpu/decoder/fetchdecode64.cc
+++ b/cpu/decoder/fetchdecode64.cc
@@ -863,7 +863,10 @@ int decoder_vex64(const Bit8u *iptr, unsigned &remain, bxInstruction_c *i, unsig
 
   ia_opcode = findOpcode(BxOpcodeTableVEX[opcode_byte], decmask);
 
-  bool has_immediate = (opcode_byte >= 0x70 && opcode_byte <= 0x73) || (opcode_byte >= 0xC2 && opcode_byte <= 0xC6) || (opcode_byte >= 0x200);
+  // Only map 1's 0F 70-73 / C2-C6 groups, every map 3 (0F 3A) opcode and map 7's imm32 forms
+  // carry an immediate. Map 5 (AMX-FP8, AMX-TRANSPOSE's T2RPNTLVWZ*RS) sits at 0x300.. in the
+  // table and has none — an unbounded ">= 0x200" fetched one byte too many there.
+  bool has_immediate = (opcode_byte >= 0x70 && opcode_byte <= 0x73) || (opcode_byte >= 0xC2 && opcode_byte <= 0xC6) || (opcode_byte >= 0x200 && opcode_byte < 0x300) || (vex_opc_map == 7);
   if (has_immediate) {
     if (vex_opc_map == 7) {
       if (remain > 3) {
